045417] [17592186443541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443541 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe722847-9990-513e-91c0-d8fbc2f2c414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\sqrt{6}t,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1147fbce-b0d6-511f-b878-2ccccfbc032b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443542 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=7t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{7}) \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,2\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{s}{7},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}s}{7},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{7})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{7})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}s}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{7}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{5}{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,-\\frac{5}{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{7})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=\\frac{9}{2}{t}^{2}.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parametrization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $y$-component of $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16cdbada-67e8-5de1-a9af-81171c0d9ab3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443543 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=\\frac{9}{2}{t}^{2},$ we have that $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})+3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2s}}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})-\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})+\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $y$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2s})+\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2s}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=12t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ffd9e9c-d842-5a8a-82a2-58996823be61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443544 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=12t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{12}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{12}) \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{s}{12}),\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{s}{12})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve  $r(t)=(3t-1)\\,i+2t\\,j+\\sqrt{3}t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=4t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c7e6c99-f8ae-5d4a-9e7f-5c595417e9ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443545 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=4t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{4}.$  \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{4}) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot \\frac{s}{4}-1)i+(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{4})j+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{4})k \\\\  & =(\\frac{3s-4}{4})i+\\frac{s}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s-4}{4})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{2})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{4})k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,1-{t}^{2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ba59ca0-21f4-55ed-9a11-93e0f28f7d7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443546 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2} & =s \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2s}{3} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{3}}) \\\\  & =⟨{(\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{2}+3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{2}\\,,1-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2s}{3}+3,\\,\\frac{s}{3},\\,1-\\frac{2s}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s}{3}+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1-\\frac{2s}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,j-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,k,$  $\\,t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a07b5060-561f-5345-8fe4-ea776cd5863a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443547 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))k \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,i-4\\cdot 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j+3\\cdot 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,k \\\\  & =20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,i-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))}^{2}+{(16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))}^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{400{cos}^{2}⁡(4t)+256{sin}^{2}⁡(4t)+144{sin}^{2}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{400[{cos}^{2}⁡(4t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(4t)]} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}20\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =20u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =20t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $s=20t$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =20t \\\\ t & =\\frac{s}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{20}) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{s}{20})i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{s}{20})j-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{s}{20})k \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})j-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve  $r(t)=(6t-6)\\,i+4(t-1)^{3/2}\\,j+2\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=4{t}^{3/2}.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parameterization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $x$-component of $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d79c5095-b4c8-55c2-88ef-92a621d4625c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443548 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since  $s=4{t}^{3/2}$,  we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =4{t}^{3/2} \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{s}{4} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =[6{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-6]\\,i+4{[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =6[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]\\,i+4{[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $x$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n6[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3/2},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3/2},\\,1⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f674176f-f4e0-51c5-b4b9-0830e520af3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443549 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2},\\,2{t}^{1/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2})}^{2}+{(2{t}^{1/2})}^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}t+4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}} & =s \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3s}{5} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{3s}{5})}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({(\\frac{3s}{5})}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =⟨{({(\\frac{3s}{5})}^{2/3})}^{3/2},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{({(\\frac{3s}{5})}^{2/3})}^{3/2},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3s}{5},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,\\frac{4s}{5},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
